#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 840.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162964} :activity/id #uuid "5e1abfbf-6305-5be0-9393-a812bd1f7fea" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049812 :topic/id #uuid "1599ff71-6ac6-594e-a9d9-6e787e12bce6" :topic/math-academy-id 3009} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162964 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163271 :tutorial/content "Let $X_1$ and $X_2$ be *independent* random variables that are normally distributed with\n\n$$\nX_1\\sim N(\\mu_1, \\sigma_1^2), \\qquad X_2\\sim N(\\mu_2, \\sigma_2^2).\n$$\n\nWe can show that the random variable $Y = X_1\\pm X_2$ is normally distributed, and\n\n$$\nY =  X_1\\pm X_2 \\sim N(\\mu_1\\pm\\mu_2, \\sigma_1^2+\\sigma_2^2).\n$$\n\nWe'll prove this in a separate lesson.\n\nFor example, if $X_1\\sim N(2,3)$ and $X_2\\sim  N(1,4)$ are independent, then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}_{1}+{X}_{2} & ∼N(2+1,3+4)∼N(3,7),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}_{1}-{X}_{2} & ∼N(2-1,3+4)∼N(1,7).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWhen computing a sum or difference of normal random variables, there are a few things to watch out for:\n\n- Firstly, when we compute the distribution of a *difference*, we subtract the means, but *add* the variances!\n\n- Secondly, it is vital to check that $X_1$ and $X_2$ are independent. Cases where $X_1$ and $X_2$ are *dependent* will be dealt with in future lessons." :tutorial/id #uuid "081629c5-dd0b-5541-b151-16f392fb9a31" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5442 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "1f2e7312-035a-58f0-a5f4-a7a2cb453deb" :step/math-academy-id 33902 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162970}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162970 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162055 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161855 :question/id #uuid "e100670a-00a3-5b89-a454-aef34af2caac" :question/math-academy-id "e-9103"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4cac2a0-2591-548f-ba83-1c55554eb499" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048615} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049412} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049815}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162270} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162289} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496259} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496260} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496285} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496327}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Distribution of a Sum or Difference of Normal Random Variables"} :step/id #uuid "b6d57cdd-23b8-5334-ab35-b2d652c0c0d2" :step/math-academy-id 33933 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163003}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162978 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162059 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161859 :question/id #uuid "934c72e0-0fa3-55db-955a-eae8d546315e" :question/math-academy-id "e-9117"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "06dabd63-8400-57cb-8978-54f0b4640759" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162261} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162271} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496222} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496225} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496254} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496291} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514461}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Distribution of a Sum or Difference of Scaled Normal Random Variables"} :step/id #uuid "e329fb62-17bf-50ce-9656-dcfb745d9da2" :step/math-academy-id 33983 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162979}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162979 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162060 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161860 :question/id #uuid "744539e8-4bb1-57c4-acaf-ed8da68650bd" :question/math-academy-id "e-9118"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4358607d-b068-5dd9-8b35-46dc6bcecd1a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050116}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162272} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162290} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496267} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496278} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496316} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496332} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514411}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Probability Involving a Sum or Difference of Normally Distributed Random Variables"} :step/id #uuid "c790b092-ebdf-54f1-8b69-54a1a21f5eb3" :step/math-academy-id 33986 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162980}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162980 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162061 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161861 :question/id #uuid "0f84847c-0ffd-573b-ac60-c7ab7633b6ea" :question/math-academy-id "e-9119"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "22f7e940-b159-5d68-8ce4-d33f4a2f6907" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050198}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162273} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162297} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496228} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496268} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496301} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496325} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514412} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514443}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Probability Involving a Sum or Difference of Normally Distributed Random Variables in Context"} :step/id #uuid "271cebaa-3f3c-5886-aecd-e3ab160bb06b" :step/math-academy-id 33988} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163003 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163285 :tutorial/content "If $X_1\\sim N(\\mu_1, \\sigma_1^2)$ and $X_2\\sim N(\\mu_2, \\sigma_2^2)$ are *independent* random variables and $a$ and $b$ are constants, we have\n\n$$\naX_1+bX_2\\sim N(a\\mu_1+b\\mu_2, a^2\\sigma^2_1+b^2\\sigma^2_2).\n$$\n\nFor example, if $X_1\\sim N(2,3)$ and $X_2\\sim  N(1,4)$ are independent, then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{X}_{1}+3{X}_{2} & ∼N(2\\cdot 2+3\\cdot 1,{2}^{2}\\cdot 3+{3}^{2}\\cdot 4) \\\\  & ∼N(4+3,4\\cdot 3+9\\cdot 4) \\\\  & ∼N(7,48),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{X}_{1}-3{X}_{2} & ∼N(2\\cdot 2+(-3)\\cdot 1,{2}^{2}\\cdot 3+{3}^{2}\\cdot 4) \\\\  & ∼N(4-3,4\\cdot 3+9\\cdot 4) \\\\  & ∼N(1,48).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e34c3ff4-0506-5301-a7a5-0d4eea0a0ed0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5523 :tutorial/title "Finding a Sum or Difference of Scaled Normal Random Variables"} :step/id #uuid "7ac39654-356a-5d2e-b561-f1ca3ba76bcb" :step/math-academy-id 34366 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162978}}] :activity/title "Combining Two Normally Distributed Random Variables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186162166}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514486} :activity/id #uuid "77b5c58d-d4ec-5f66-9f2a-e4e55aab433c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049812 :topic/id #uuid "1599ff71-6ac6-594e-a9d9-6e787e12bce6" :topic/math-academy-id 3009} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514486 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162271 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d11b7231-0057-57a9-be1e-6caff3c8a1d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "N(-20,-35)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158830} {:answer/id #uuid "31e46495-af3d-5634-a059-e1014e45dee4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "N(40,315)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513666} {:answer/id #uuid "b488c945-d6ec-5534-9219-1a79a5b81aab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "N(-20,315)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514004} {:answer/id #uuid "d878f46f-c745-5568-b119-bf0d3db97f4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "N(-20,75)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514096} {:answer/id #uuid "d9180bb9-7dcb-558b-87f6-8eb5ecb22a62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "N(40,75)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514101}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514004} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d383e28-f11d-5b7d-99bb-ce01b2ea2599" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158828}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "12412d67-5cf4-5443-9bb6-49897b9665a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-108970" :question/problem "Suppose that $X$ and $Y$ are independent random variables such that $X∼N(5,10)$ and $Y∼N(6,11).$ Find the distribution of the random variable $W=2X-5Y.$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent random variables, where ${X}_{1}∼N({\\mu }_{1},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2})$ and ${X}_{2}∼N({\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2})$, and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then \n\n$$\na{X}_{1}+b{X}_{2}∼N(a{\\mu }_{1}+b{\\mu }_{2},{a}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{b}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}).\n$$\n\nSince $X$ and $Y$ are independent, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W=2X-5Y & ∼N(2\\cdot 5+(-5)\\cdot 6,{2}^{2}\\cdot 10+(-5)^{2}\\cdot 11) \\\\  & ∼N(-20,315).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, $W∼N(-20,315).$"} :step/id #uuid "0526a648-ee54-5a70-b790-abd5fc388aa7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514522}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514488 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514412 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2011937d-5a96-5735-baa1-c9d00e438e53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.4292" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513618} {:answer/id #uuid "912af71c-ec6c-5acf-a218-4c537f9cb75e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.3944" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513908} {:answer/id #uuid "ac32782d-5d6e-552b-9ecc-ae443cf9c2be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.4015" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513984} {:answer/id #uuid "bca9eba0-cb3e-5af7-8eb5-bc98388caaf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.3980" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514033} {:answer/id #uuid "edf7bc56-279f-5326-8543-b3671134dc41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.4131" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514153}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513908} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb070c91-e2b2-5f1b-b37c-ab7d04905344" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514313}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "666db65e-d2dd-5df6-aa61-3e935a25f26e" :question/math-academy-id "q-109755" :question/problem "A factory produces shovels, each consisting of a handle and a blade.\nThe length of a handle is normally distributed with a mean of $120$ centimeters and a variance of $0.14,$  while the length of a blade is normally distributed with a mean of $30$ centimeters and a variance of $0.02.$ If one shovel is randomly selected, what is the probability that its length is more than $150$ centimeters, but less than $150.5$ centimeters? You may assume that the lengths are independent.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ | $\\,0.09\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.8944\\,$ | $\\,0.8962\\,$ | $\\,0.8980\\,$ | $\\,0.8997\\,$ | $\\,0.9015\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.3\\,$ | $\\,0.9115\\,$ | $\\,0.9131\\,$ | $\\,0.9147\\,$ | $\\,0.9162\\,$ | $\\,0.9177\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.4\\,$ | $\\,0.9265\\,$ | $\\,0.9279\\,$ | $\\,0.9292\\,$ | $\\,0.9306\\,$ | $\\,0.9319\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ denote the length of a handle, and let $Y$ denote the length of a blade. We want to find \n\n$$\nP(150<X+Y<150.5).\n$$\n\nSince $X$ and $Y$ are independent random variables such that $X∼N(120,0.14)$ and $Y∼N(30,0.02)$, then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X+Y & ∼N(120+30,0.14+0.02) \\\\  & ∼N(150,0.16).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the variable $X+Y$ is normally distributed with mean $\\mu =150$ and standard deviation $\\sigma =\\sqrt{0.16}=0.4.$\n\nIn order to find $P(150<X+Y<150.5),$ we convert $X+Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(150<X+Y<150.5) & =P(\\frac{150-150}{0.4}<Z<\\frac{150.5-150}{0.4}) \\\\  & ≈P(0<Z<1.25) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 1.25)-P(Z\\le 0) \\\\  & =Φ(1.25)-Φ(0)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(1.25) & ≈0.8944,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(0) & =0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(150<X+Y<150.5) & ≈Φ(1.25)-Φ(0) \\\\  & ≈0.8944-0.5 \\\\  & ≈0.3944.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that the probability that the length of a randomly selected shovel is more than $150$ centimeters, but less than $150.5$ centimeters is $0.3944.$"} :step/id #uuid "09e477d4-799c-59d3-9b5b-d55710b82507" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514539}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514522 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514411 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f2a22da-8ee7-5c03-9406-a381bafad62e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.92" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513698} {:answer/id #uuid "67813379-2e23-5017-8ad9-a1a5d968c331" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.89" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513810} {:answer/id #uuid "82b05498-2c78-5f43-a343-c6dea0a978f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.87" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513873} {:answer/id #uuid "d2c794a4-ad11-517b-b863-482edd911ccf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.90" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514085} {:answer/id #uuid "f908b992-6ecb-5dd9-87da-ddd849714903" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.91" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514183}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513810} :answer-field/id #uuid "63dec131-ea37-5a89-8bfa-e5223fe88c95" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514275}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "65fa3d9f-f374-57d7-bfef-bf8f42da2e6e" :question/math-academy-id "q-109732" :question/problem "If ${X}_{1}∼N(5,3)$ and ${X}_{2}∼N(8,4)$ are independent and $Y=4{X}_{1}-2{X}_{2},$ calculate $P(Y<14).$ Round your final answer to two decimal places.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ | $\\,0.09\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.1\\,$ | $\\,0.8749\\,$ | $\\,0.8770\\,$ | $\\,0.8790\\,$ | $\\,0.8810\\,$ | $\\,0.8830\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.8944\\,$ | $\\,0.8962\\,$ | $\\,0.8980\\,$ | $\\,0.8997\\,$ | $\\,0.9015\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.3\\,$ | $\\,0.9115\\,$ | $\\,0.9131\\,$ | $\\,0.9147\\,$ | $\\,0.9162\\,$ | $\\,0.9177\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent random variables, where ${X}_{1}∼N({\\mu }_{1},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2})$ and ${X}_{2}∼N({\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}),$ and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then \n\n$$\na{X}_{1}+b{X}_{2}∼N(a{\\mu }_{1}+b{\\mu }_{2},{a}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{b}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}).\n$$\n\nSince ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent, the distribution of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y=4{X}_{1}-2{X}_{2} & ∼N(4{\\mu }_{1}-2{\\mu }_{2},16{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+4{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}) \\\\  & ∼N(20-16,48+16) \\\\  & ∼N(4,64).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Y$ is normally distributed with mean $\\mu =4$ and standard deviation $\\sigma =\\sqrt{64}=8.$ \n\nWe want to find $P(Y<14).$ Thus, we convert $Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y<14) & =P(Z<\\frac{14-4}{8}) \\\\  & =P(Z<1.25) \\\\  & =Φ(1.25)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(1.25)≈0.8944.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(Y<14)≈0.89.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a6c520d3-89e0-5390-82da-a20a42247402" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514541}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514539 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514461 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41d541ba-49ec-5748-874b-c3423e6862f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Y∼N(0,7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513706} {:answer/id #uuid "69890d9b-b5ce-57f5-9e6b-f58c9ce8bd33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Y∼N(0,36)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513814} {:answer/id #uuid "8012c74a-5d13-52a6-a453-e6796e086a62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Not enough information to determine the answer" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513865} {:answer/id #uuid "b4186d1e-6606-5fe8-9ade-023864d3dde7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Y∼N(0,19)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514003} {:answer/id #uuid "dc202adc-0608-5335-955f-0d38c624c45c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Y∼N(2,7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514108}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514003} :answer-field/id #uuid "9fffc01b-d9f2-5f94-b352-b2003ad83619" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514302}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c53663ac-ef5b-58bc-a1be-8cea3868fa2f" :question/math-academy-id "q-175410" :question/problem "If ${X}_{1}∼N(-3,1)$ and ${X}_{2}∼N(1,2)$ are independent, then what is the distribution of $Y={X}_{1}+3{X}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent random variables, where ${X}_{1}∼N({\\mu }_{1},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2})$ and ${X}_{2}∼N({\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}),$ and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then \n\n$$\na{X}_{1}+b{X}_{2}∼N(a{\\mu }_{1}+b{\\mu }_{2},{a}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{b}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}).\n$$\n\nSince ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y={X}_{1}+3{X}_{2} & ∼N(1\\cdot (-3)+3\\cdot 1,{1}^{2}\\cdot 1+{3}^{2}\\cdot 2) \\\\  & ∼N(0,19).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Y∼N(0,19).$"} :step/id #uuid "dad6d94b-b8a2-50a2-a3b8-ab747acac997"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514541 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514443 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b7ed843-f01b-5ff0-9400-1f575ae46bc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.4817" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513604} {:answer/id #uuid "3c7b928a-dbf0-5c53-8e9c-221cc73604b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.4925" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513688} {:answer/id #uuid "953e7d4e-e884-5c5e-bebe-5d6c15fc8f60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.4639" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513919} {:answer/id #uuid "a7650dd9-8eb4-5963-be58-a9d76c3de7ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.5153" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513965} {:answer/id #uuid "c072162b-92cd-5039-9d7e-cf458cf7fc3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.5003" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514040}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513688} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f73640d-d4b8-5379-a9d2-f83257d2b179" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514271}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "9dd9198d-6665-5e5e-bf17-48bb8a7b15be" :question/math-academy-id "q-109748" :question/problem "The weights of some chocolate cookies are normally distributed with a mean of $10\\,\\text{g}$ and a variance of $1\\,{\\text{g}}^{2}.$ The weights of some oatcakes are normally distributed with a mean of $15\\,\\text{g}$ and a variance of $2\\,{\\text{g}}^{2}.$  If one chocolate cookie and one oatcake are randomly selected, what is the probability that $6$ times the weight of the cookie plus $4$ times the weight of oatcake is less than $120\\,\\text{g}$, but more than $100\\,\\text{g}?$ You may assume that the weights of the cookies and oatcakes are independent. \n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-2.5\\,$ | $\\,0.0062\\,$ | $\\,0.0060\\,$ | $\\,0.0059\\,$ | $\\,0.0057\\,$ | $\\,0.0055\\,$ |\n| $\\,-2.4\\,$ | $\\,0.0082\\,$ | $\\,0.0080\\,$ | $\\,0.0078\\,$ | $\\,0.0075\\,$ | $\\,0.0073\\,$ |\n| $\\,-2.3\\,$ | $\\,0.0107\\,$ | $\\,0.0104\\,$ | $\\,0.0102\\,$ | $\\,0.0099\\,$ | $\\,0.0096\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ denote the weight of a chocolate cookie, and let $Y$ denote the weight of an oatcake. We want to find \n\n$$\nP(100<6X+4Y<120).\n$$\n\nSince $X$ and $Y$ are  independent random variables such that $X∼N(10,1)$ and $Y∼N(15,2),$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6X+4Y & ∼N(6\\cdot 10+4\\cdot 15,36\\cdot 1+16\\cdot 2) \\\\  & ∼N(120,68).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the variable $6X+4Y$ is normally distributed with mean $\\mu =120$ and standard deviation $\\sigma =\\sqrt{68}.$\n\nTo find $P(100<6X+4Y<120),$ we convert $6X+4Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(100<6X+4Y<120) & =P(\\frac{100-120}{\\sqrt{68}}<Z<\\frac{120-120}{\\sqrt{68}}) \\\\  & ≈P(-2.43<Z<0) \\\\  & =P(Z<0)-P(Z\\le -2.43) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 0)-P(Z\\le -2.43) \\\\  & =Φ(0)-Φ(-2.43)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-2.43) & ≈0.0075,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(0) & =0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(100<6X+4Y<120) & ≈Φ(0)-Φ(-2.43) \\\\  & =0.5-0.0075 \\\\  & ≈0.4925.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "e71a59e7-528e-5195-bc2d-e6704ffb5651" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514488}}] :activity/title "Combining Two Normally Distributed Random Variables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513517}]}
